# 7. Privacy-Enhancing Technologies (PETs)

## Aim

To study Privacy-Enhancing Technologies (PETs) and understand how they protect users' privacy, reduce unnecessary exposure of personal information, and improve security during digital activities.

## Introduction

Privacy-Enhancing Technologies (PETs) are technologies and techniques designed to protect personal information while allowing users to access digital services. PETs can reduce tracking, protect communications, hide network information, encrypt data, or limit the amount of information exposed to third parties.

Common examples include **VPNs, Tor, end-to-end encrypted messaging, Private DNS, encryption, and privacy-focused browsers**. Each technology protects a different part of the digital communication process. Therefore, using one PET does not automatically provide complete privacy.

---

## Objectives

1. To understand the concept of Privacy-Enhancing Technologies.
2. To study different technologies used for protecting user privacy.
3. To understand how VPN, Tor, encryption and other PETs work.
4. To compare their advantages and limitations.
5. To identify appropriate use cases for different PETs.
6. To understand the importance of combining security and privacy technologies.

