# 3. End-to-End Encrypted Messaging

**End-to-end encryption (E2EE)** protects messages so that only the communicating endpoints are intended to read their contents.

### Privacy Mechanism

Messages are encrypted on the sender's device and decrypted on the recipient's device. The service provider generally cannot read the plaintext contents when E2EE is correctly implemented.

### Advantages

* Protects message contents from intermediaries.
* Useful for private conversations.
* Protects communications against many forms of interception.
* Can provide strong confidentiality when implemented correctly.

### Limitations

* Metadata may still be exposed depending on the application.
* The security of the endpoint devices is important.
* Backups may have different privacy properties.
* Users can still reveal information by forwarding, copying or taking screenshots.

### Appropriate Use Cases

* Personal communication
* Business communication
* Sharing confidential information
* Sensitive group conversations

